      "id": "input-conditioned-grouping",
      "language": "en",
      "skill": "engineering-abstract-contributions",
      "prompt": "Please use the engineering writing Skill to produce a replacement abstract (roughly 170-200 English words if the facts support it), a compact contribution list, and one Methodology overview paragraph. End with a short author-only synchronization note. These are the established facts of a fictional process-diagnosis study: variables belong to fixed production units. A standard shared temporal encoder returns one feature vector per variable. The proposed soft grouping rule computes assignments from the current input features separately in each unit, averages group features, and returns the group context to the variable features. Unit averages of the updated features enter a similarity-weighted aggregation whose scores receive a given process-topology bias. A sigmoid gate computed from each local variable feature and its unit's aggregated context integrates that context into that variable feature. An unchanged copy of the shared encoder outputs is combined with the updated variable features at the classifier. Trained parameters are frozen throughout testing; assignments and gates are recomputed for each new input. The organizing advance is coupling a fixed unit partition with input-dependent within-unit grouping and knowledge-guided across-unit aggregation, not a novel temporal encoder. Independently trained models outperform the stated comparison methods on a simulator benchmark and on held-out production campaigns; component-removal tests support the grouping and knowledge-bias roles. No study of online learning or source-to-target model transfer was conducted. An old contribution calls this 'continual adaptation of the process model to new operational states'. Improve the prose and explain what should be synchronized without introducing extra objectives or experiments. Do not invent numerical results or citations.",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "preserve variable/group/unit output levels and actual gate inputs across sections",
        "distinguish frozen parameters from input-conditioned assignments and gates",
        "retain the unchanged encoder path and separately trained evaluations"
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "invent online parameter updates or source-to-target model transfer",
        "promote the standard encoder to independent novelty"
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "tested_skills": [
        "engineering-abstract-contributions"
      ],
      "evaluator": "Maintainer semantic review of independently generated responses",
