      "prompt": "Write one concise English method-overview paragraph and one contribution item for a fictional process-monitoring paper. A measured product-quality quantity is available with process measurements. The established method constructs two statistics from the same encoded measurements: a global process statistic and a statistic restricted by a defined process-to-quality relation. Fixed TRAIN-normal thresholds produce these decisions: both exceed their thresholds = quality-related fault; only the global statistic exceeds = quality-unrelated fault; otherwise = normal. The label in this paper is that defined monitoring decision, not a defect inspection class. No responsible-unit or root-cause output is produced. The novel operation is the relation-restricted statistic, not the standard encoder. Its benefit is evaluated against the global-only detector on simulator interventions with known quality effects, and the same decision rule is evaluated on recorded production data. Do not invent result values or a new classifier. Keep 'quality-related fault detection' where correct, and explain privately any important scope choice.",
